'''trebble†''', ''a.'', ''n.'', and ''v.'' An obsolete spelling of ''treble''.

¶
'''treble''' (treb′l), ''a.'' and ''n.'' [Early mod. E. ''trebble''; < ME. ''treble'', ''tribill'', < OF. ''treble'', ''treible'', triple, < L. ''triplus'', threefold: see ''triple'', of which ''treble'' is a doublet.] '''I.''' ''a.'' '''1.''' Threefold; triple.

{{smaller block|Regall estate, coucht in the ''treble'' crowne,<br>Ancestrell all, by linage and by right.<br>{{right|''Puttenham'', Partheniades, iii.}}}}

{{smaller block|A skull hid in the earth a ''treble'' age<br>Shall sooner prate. {{right|''Ford'', Broken Heart, v. 1.}}}}

'''2.''' In ''music'', pertaining to the voice or the voice-part called treble or soprano; high in

readings.c2s1:
pitch; in harmony, occupying the upper place: as, a ''treble'' voice; a ''treble'' violin. See II.

{{smaller block|The case of a ''treble'' hautboy.
{{right|''Shak.'', 2 Hen. IV., iii. 2. 351.}}}}

{{smaller block|Unto the violl they danct; . . .
Then bespake the ''treble'' string, . . .
"O yonder is my father the king."
{{right|''The Miller and the King's Daughter'' (Child's Ballads, II. 359).}}}}

{{smaller block|Bob spoke with a sharp and rather ''treble'' volubility.
{{right|''George Eliot'', Mill on the Floss, iii. 6.}}}}

{{smaller|'''Cottised treble.''' See ''cottised.''—'''Treble clef,''' in ''musical notation'', either a soprano clef (that is, a C clef on the first line of a staff) or a violin-clef (that is, a G clef on the second line). See ''clef'' and ''staff.''—'''Treble coursing,''' in ''mining'', the expansion of a ventilating current into three currents or courses.—'''Treble cross-staff,''' in ''her.'', a crozier triple-crossed, or having the papal cross.—'''Treble fitché.''' See ''fitché.''}}

'''II.''' ''n.'' 1. In ''music:'' (''a'') Same as ''soprano'' (which see). {{smaller|The term arose from the fact that in early contrapuntal music the chief melody or cantus firmus was given to the tenor (which see), and the voice-parts added above were called respectively the ''discantus'' or alto and the ''treble'' (that is, 'third' part) or soprano.}}

{{smaller block|''Hor.'' Madam, my instrument's in tune.
''Bian.'' Let's hear. O fie! the ''treble'' jars.
{{right|''Shak.'', T. of the S., iii. 1. 39.}}}}

{{smaller block|Maidenlike, as far
As I could ape their ''treble'', did I sing.
{{right|''Tennyson'', Princess, iv.}}}}

(''b'') A singer with a soprano or treble voice, or an instrument that takes the upper part in concerted music.

