{{smaller block|"Nay, Will," quod that wyȝt, "wend thou no ferther,
But lyue as this lyf is ordeyned for the;
Thou tomblest with a ''trepget'' ȝif thou my tras folwe."
{{right|''Piers Plowman'' (A), xii. 91.}}}}

{{smaller block|Withoute stroke it mote be take
Of ''trepeget'' or mangonel.
{{right|''Rom. of the Rose'', l. 6279.}}}}

2. A kind of balance or scales used in weighing coins or other small articles, the pan containing which tilts over if the balance is not exact.

{{smaller block|The French pattern of ''trebuchet'', or tilting scale, now largely manufactured here.
{{right|''Lea'', Photography, p. 420.}}}}

3. A kind of trap for catching small birds or animals by the tilting of the part on which the bait is placed.—4. A cucking-stool.

{{smaller block|She [a common scold] may be indicted, and, if convicted, shall be sentenced to be placed in a certain engine of correction called the ''trebucket'', castigatory, or cucking-stool.
{{right|''Blackstone'', Com., IV. xiii.}}}}

'''trebucket†''', ''n.'' Same as ''trebuchet.''

